@meta
id: 09-09
title: Delete as far as a character
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 16
minutes: 4
vscode: click before the argument, shift-click after the comma, Delete
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The payment gateway dropped the `amount` parameter from `refund` in the
last release — it reads the amount off the order now. The call here still
passes `order.total` and the build is failing on it.

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total, 'MANUAL');

Make it pass `order.id` and `'MANUAL'` only, spaced as the other calls in
this file are, and save.

@keys
| `f,` | forward onto the next comma |
| `df,` | delete from the cursor up to and including the next comma |
| `d2f,` | the same, but to the second comma |

@hints
Counting words through `order.total,` is a trap — the dot and the comma
are words too. Delete to a *character* instead.
--
`f` is a motion, so `d` takes it. `df,` deletes everything from the
cursor through the next comma inclusive.
--
Land on the space after the first comma, then `df,`. From line one:
`4j`, `f,`, `l`, `df,`.

@solution
    4j f, l df, :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes for an edit that VS Code charges you a click, a
shift-click and a good aim for.

Two things make this work. `f` is inclusive — it takes the character it
lands on — so `df,` removes the comma as well as the argument. And
starting one character to the right of the first comma means the space
that belonged to the deleted argument goes with it, leaving the spacing
of the remaining arguments already correct. Landing on the comma itself
and pressing `df,` would leave you with two spaces to tidy up.

That choice, cursor one character left or right before an operator, is
most of what separates a clean edit from one you have to go back and fix.

`f` also takes a count, and the count survives the operator: `d2f,`
deletes through the second comma from here. If you ever want to strip two
arguments in one go, that is the key.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total, 'MANUAL');

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id } });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, 'MANUAL');

    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id } });
  }
}
